#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
"""Write a CycloneDX 1.5 SBOM for one system image.

Usage:
    image-sbom --system NAME --board BOARD --config CONFIG --dir DIR
               --sdk DIR --pds DIR --toolchain CHANNEL --std-crates FILE
               --out FILE
               [--target TRIPLE] [--cargo CARGO] [--components FILE]
               [--containerfile FILE] [--position FILE]
               [--sdcard-config FILE --uboot-defconfig NAME
                --uboot-fragment FILE --firmware FILE [--uboot-bin FILE]]
               [--unit-graph FILE --metadata FILE] [--arch arm64|amd64]
               [--glyphs DIR]

`make image` runs this once the Microkit tool has made DIR/loader.img (DIR
is build/BOARD/SYSTEM), and writes FILE beside it. The SBOM names what
loader.img is made of, each part with its sha256:

* the Microkit SDK's kernel, loader, monitor and capDL initialiser for BOARD
  and CONFIG (board/BOARD/CONFIG/elf/ in the SDK), with their versions and
  licences from the reviewed components file (tools/image-components.toml);
* every program image the system description names (DIR/SYSTEM.system, as
  tools/board-system wrote it for BOARD): the ELF the image was made from
  and the protection domains that run it;
* every crate compiled into a program image, with the dependency graph. The
  crates come from cargo's own unit graph for the build `make pds` runs -
  the same packages, profile and features - so a crate is listed exactly
  when it is compiled for the device (TRIPLE, default aarch64-sel4). That
  includes the standard-library crates build-std compiles, which must be on
  the reviewed list (FILE, pds/std-crates.txt);
* the system description itself and any prefill files;
* the glyph atlases' notice, OFL.txt, which `make image` puts beside every
  image: the copyright line and licence of the font the
  atlases are a Modified Version of, with that font - family, version, file
  and sha256 - from the manifest the atlas tool writes
  (DIR/src/atlas/manifest.toml, DIR the atlas crate, --glyphs). When a
  program image links the atlas crate, its component (licence OFL-1.1, from
  cargo metadata) names the font too.

What built the image but is not in it - build scripts, proc macros and what
they need, and the Microkit tool - is listed as `excluded`, with the reason.
Each crate's licence and source come from cargo metadata and Cargo.lock, as
in the workspace SBOMs (tools/sbom), with the same component names.

A part whose licence cannot be taken without a GPL-family licence is marked
as needing a source offer (nowayos:source-offer). A prebuilt binary
whose contents are not audited (composition = "unaudited" in the components
file) is marked 'composition unaudited' and listed in a composition whose
aggregate is "unknown". One whose audit is recorded (composition = "audited",
or "audited, sign-off pending" while a licence in it awaits the project
owner, and `audit` naming the audit's file: the capDL initialiser, audited in
tools/initialiser-audit.toml) holds every crate the audit found in
it as a nested component, each with its licence and where its sign-off
stands, in a composition whose aggregate is "complete". The marker must
match the audit: "audited" is refused while any sign-off is pending, and a
crate whose licence needs more than the house list (MIT, Apache-2.0,
BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, ISC) is refused unless it names its sign-off or
says it is pending (not both). An audit holds only for the Microkit SDK
(version and both archives' sha256) and architecture it names: with any
other SDK pinned, the part is 'composition unaudited' again, and nothing in
its entry points to the audit's crates, until the audit is re-run.

With --sdcard-config and the U-Boot and firmware options (make passes them
for rpi5b_2gb, which boots from an SD card) the SBOM also lists what `make
sdcard` puts beside loader.img: U-Boot, built from the source release the
Containerfile pins (with u-boot.bin's sha256 when --uboot-bin names it, as
make sdcard does), every firmware file with the sha256 FILE pins
(tools/container/rpi-firmware.sha256), and config.txt.

The output is a pure function of its inputs: no timestamp or absolute path,
and everything sorted, so two runs on one build give the same bytes. It is
checked against the CycloneDX 1.5 structure (tools/sbom's validate) before
it is written. --unit-graph and --metadata read cargo's output from files
instead of running cargo, and --arch names the host architecture whose SDK
archive the Containerfile pins (default: this machine's); the tests use them.

Exit status: 0 written; 1 the SBOM failed its own validation; 2 usage or
input error. Python 3.11 or later (tomllib), standard library only.
"""

